Harmonic analysis is a "divide and conquer" game and orthogonality is everywhere in this game. The classical idea is to divide one function into many orthogonal pieces and prove inequalities for each piece, then put back together using their orthogonality. A new recently developed topic is to give many functions which are orthonormal, and explore the sharp inequalities for these family. This type of inequalities have applications in many-body problems and related to a central conjecture, Kakeya conjecture in geometric theory and harmonic analysis.


This five-day workshop in Hangzhou will bring together specialists in harmonic analysis, spectral theory, operator theory, and partial differential equations from all over the world to discuss the recent delevopements on the orthogonality in harmonic analysis. They will study how families of orthonormal functions concentrate, disperse, and respond to changes in geometry or physical potentials. This workshop aims to attract and benefit young researchers to work on some open problems in this field.

