Orthogonality is a fundamental tool in harmonic analysis: many classical results are proven by decomposing a function into its mutually orthogonal components, deriving estimates for the individual pieces, and then exploiting orthogonality to recover information about the original function. 
This idea has led to advances in spectral theory and the theory of partial differential equations, such as Fourier restriction estimates and uniform resolvent estimates.

A more recent and rapidly developing direction considers inequalities for entire orthonormal families of functions, seeking the best possible bounds in this broader setting.
These inequalities arise naturally in many-body problems and have deep connections with major questions in geometric measure theory and harmonic analysis, including the Kakeya conjecture.

Lieb and Thirring~\cite{LiebThirring1975} first introduced these powerful functional inequalities for systems of orthonormal functions in their seminal proof of the stability of matter in quantum mechanics.
These are generalizations of Sobolev-type inequalities. In recent years, a major trend has been to extend the Lieb-Thirring philosophy to other cornerstone inequalities in harmonic analysis, such as Strichartz inequalities~\cite{MR512086}, the Stein–Tomas inequality \cite{MR358216} or Sogge's spectral cluster bounds~\cite{MR930395}. The main challenge is to obtain estimates with an optimal dependence on the number of functions, which is crucial in applications, e.g., to many-body quantum systems. 

Orthogonality in Harmonic Analysis: Old and New will bring together classical perspectives and contemporary developments, showcasing the central role of orthogonality and its connections with geometry, mathematical physics, and related areas.
